/**
 * The map widget's basemap (ROADMAP Canvas item 4) — country outlines, in the
 * bundle.
 *
 * **Why not tiles.** Every mapping library defaults to raster tiles from a
 * third-party host, and that default is wrong for this product specifically.
 * Anchor deploys into the customer's own AWS account, frequently into a VPC
 * with no egress; a tile layer makes the *viewer's browser* call out to
 * someone else's server on every pan, and each of those requests carries the
 * viewport — which, on a map of the customer's own sites, vehicles or
 * incidents, is a fair approximation of where their data is. Bundling the
 * outline costs ~60 KB once, works with no network at all, and asks nobody to
 * accept that trade without being told.
 *
 * **What that costs, stated plainly.** There is no street-level detail and
 * there never will be from this file: at 1:110m, zooming past country scale
 * shows pins against blank space. `map.tsx` says so on screen at high zoom
 * rather than letting an empty background read as "no data here". A
 * deployment that genuinely wants detailed tiles wants a *configurable* tile
 * URL pointing at a host the customer chose (their own tile server, or their
 * own account with a commercial provider) — the extension point is named in
 * STATUS rather than half-built here.
 *
 * Data: Natural Earth 1:110m Admin 0 Countries, public domain (CC0),
 * simplified to 0.3° by `scripts/make-basemap.py` — rerun that to regenerate.
 * Coordinates are x=lon, y=-lat (SVG's y grows downward), so drawing is an
 * affine transform rather than a projection.
 */

